:1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central requested facts without a material error. It says to read the conversion notices and that timing varies, which avoids assuming a fund-specific timetable. It flags cutoff dates for purchases, redemptions, exchanges and automatic plans. It says direct-held accounts may need to move to a brokerage and advises starting an ACATS or in-kind transfer early. It covers fractional shares being cashed out, possibly taxably, and defers to the notice on treatment. On trading access, it explains that shares will be bought and sold on an exchange through a brokerage. It treats the event as a whole-fund conversion and does not confuse it with adding an ETF share class. The main omission is that it never says plainly that some fund accounts cannot hold ETF shares, or that doing nothing can leave the converted shares untradeable until a transfer is arranged. This is only implied by steps 3 and 5. The reference to a shareholder vote or proxy is unnecessary, since many conversions need none, but it is presented as something to check rather than a requirement.\"}","ttft_ms":5213,"type":"result","duration_ms":8452,"uuid":"173c3a51-5e53-4f92-bf41-3ca6a7653ac1","ttft_stream_ms":495,"time_to_request_ms":69,"first_content_frame_ms":495,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
